{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"promises","title":"Promises","group":"Asynchronous JavaScript","order":43,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A Promise is an object representing a value that will be available later — or an error. It is the foundation of modern async JavaScript.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Promise</strong> — an object in one of three states: <strong>pending</strong>, then either <strong>fulfilled</strong> with a value or <strong>rejected</strong> with a reason. Once settled it never changes.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Creating and consuming</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const wait = (ms, value) =&gt; new Promise((resolve) =&gt; setTimeout(() =&gt; resolve(value), ms));\nconst fail = (msg) =&gt; new Promise((_, reject) =&gt; setTimeout(() =&gt; reject(new Error(msg)), 5));\n\nwait(10, \"done\").then((v) =&gt; console.log(\"fulfilled:\", v));\nfail(\"boom\")\n  .then(() =&gt; console.log(\"never runs\"))\n  .catch((err) =&gt; console.log(\"rejected:\", err.message))\n  .finally(() =&gt; console.log(\"finally: cleanup either way\"));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>rejected: boom\nfinally: cleanup either way\nfulfilled: done</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link\"></i>Chaining: every then returns a new promise</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>Promise.resolve(2)\n  .then((n) =&gt; n * 10)                         // return a value → next then gets it\n  .then((n) =&gt; new Promise((r) =&gt; setTimeout(() =&gt; r(n + 1), 5)))  // return a promise → waits\n  .then((n) =&gt; {\n    console.log(\"value:\", n);\n    throw new Error(\"oops\");                   // throw → skips to the next catch\n  })\n  .then(() =&gt; console.log(\"skipped\"))\n  .catch((e) =&gt; {\n    console.log(\"caught:\", e.message);\n    return \"recovered\";                        // catch can recover the chain\n  })\n  .then((v) =&gt; console.log(\"after catch:\", v));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>value: 21\ncaught: oops\nafter catch: recovered</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Inside a <code>then</code> handler you…</th><th>The returned promise…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>return a value</td><td>fulfils with that value</td></tr><tr><td>return a promise/thenable</td><td>adopts its eventual state</td></tr><tr><td>throw</td><td>rejects with the thrown error</td></tr><tr><td>return nothing</td><td>fulfils with <code>undefined</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Common mistakes</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const getData = () =&gt; Promise.resolve({ id: 1 });\n\n// 1. forgetting to return → the next then gets undefined\ngetData()\n  .then((d) =&gt; { getData(); })\n  .then((v) =&gt; console.log(\"forgot return:\", v));\n\n// 2. the executor runs synchronously; resolve only once\nconst p = new Promise((resolve) =&gt; {\n  console.log(\"executor runs now\");\n  resolve(\"first\");\n  resolve(\"second\");                 // ignored\n});\np.then((v) =&gt; console.log(\"settled with:\", v));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>executor runs now\nsettled with: first\nforgot return: undefined</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>An unhandled rejection crashes Node (since v15) and logs an error in browsers. Every promise chain needs a <code>.catch</code> at the end (or an <code>await</code> inside <code>try/catch</code>).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tool\"></i>Promise.resolve / reject / withResolvers</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>console.log(await Promise.resolve(42));\nconst p = Promise.resolve(Promise.resolve(\"nested\"));   // promises flatten\nconsole.log(await p);\n\nconst { promise, resolve } = Promise.withResolvers();     // ES2024\nsetTimeout(() =&gt; resolve(\"resolved from outside\"), 5);\nconsole.log(await promise);\nawait Promise.reject(new Error(\"x\")).catch((e) =&gt; console.log(\"rejected with\", e.message));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>42\nnested\nresolved from outside\nrejected with x</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What are the states of a Promise?</summary><div><p>Pending, fulfilled and rejected. Fulfilled and rejected are together called <em>settled</em>; a settled promise is immutable, and handlers attached later still run (asynchronously) with the settled value.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why do promise callbacks run asynchronously even if the promise is already resolved?</summary><div><p>Handlers are always queued as microtasks, never called synchronously. This guarantees consistent ordering — code after <code>.then(...)</code> always runs before the handler, whether the promise was already settled or not.</p></div></details></section>"}